Validate the index at the point it becomes a URL, in `rawUrl`, so upload/restore/future custom-repo paths all inherit it:

```ts
const SHA = /^[0-9a-f]{40}$/;
const SEG = /^[A-Za-z0-9._~ ()'!*+,;=@$&-]+$/; // no '/', and rejected below if '..'

export function rawUrl(index: BaselineIndex, path: string): string {
  if (!SHA.test(index.commit)) throw new Error('baseline index: commit is not a full 40-character SHA');
  const segs = path.split('/');
  if (segs.some((s) => s === '' || s === '.' || s === '..' || !SEG.test(s))) {
    throw new Error(`baseline index: unsafe path segment in ${path}`);
  }
  const url = new URL(`https://raw.githubusercontent.com/${index.owner}/${index.repo}/${index.commit}/${segs.map(encodeURIComponent).join('/')}`);
  const prefix = `/${index.owner}/${index.repo}/${index.commit}/`;
  if (!url.pathname.startsWith(prefix)) throw new Error(`baseline index: path escapes the pin: ${path}`);
  return url.href;
}
```

The `url.pathname.startsWith(prefix)` re-check after construction is the part that actually closes it, because it is evaluated *after* the parser normalises. Add a test over the real `baselines/*.index.json` asserting `commit` matches `/^[0-9a-f]{40}$/` and that every entry in `files` round-trips through `rawUrl` back under the pin prefix — that file currently has zero test coverage.

